<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guanzhong</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guanzhong is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-river">Wei River</a> valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a> province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, a fertile basin enclosed by mountains and guarded passes. Its strategic strength made it the base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> state and the seat of the capitals of Western Han, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and Tang at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a>. In 206 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> divided it among three former Qin generals, the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-qins">Three Qins</a>'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guanzhong.jpg" alt="Guanzhong" width="320" height="334" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ulamm · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shudao.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
